## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design end-to-end API tests for dynamic feature flags and multi-region consistency under outages.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed config propagation latency
- Per-tenant isolation and cache invalidation
- Canary rollouts and rollback safety
- Observability and chaos testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: verify flag flip propagates across regions within TTL
async function testFlagPropagation(regions, tenantId, flagKey, expected) {
  const before = await fetchRecommendations(tenantId, regions[0]);
  await flipFlag(flagKey, true); // in etcd
  for (const r of regions) {
    const v = await pollUntilChanged(() => fetchRecommendations(tenantId, r), old => old !== before, 60000);
    if (v === before) throw new Error('Flag did not propagate in region ' + r);
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure TTL impact on user-perceived latency during flag flips?
- How would you test partial outages where some regions see new flags while others don't?

## Why This Is Asked

CDN edge caching is critical for low latency at global scale; testing must cover caching behavior and privacy across tenants, including purges and cross-region invalidation.

## Key Concepts

- Edge caching headers (Age, Cache-Control, X-Cache-Status)
- Per-tenant isolation in shared CDNs
- Purge propagation latency across regions
- Cache-bypass and TTL interactions under concurrency
- Chaos testing for edge nodes

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple cache header check
async function check(url, headers) {
  const res = await fetch(url, { headers });
  console.log(res.headers.get('X-Cache-Status'), res.headers.get('Age'));
  return await res.text();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this plan for dynamic TTLs during deploys?
- How would you test multi-tenant purge behavior with varying cache keys?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle focusing on real-time WebSocket delivery and per-tenant isolation within shared infra, complementing REST-centric tests.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant isolation in shared infra
- Idempotent REST endpoints with a WebSocket delivery path
- Backpressure, reconnect/backoff, and end-to-end latency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal WebSocket client test for in-order sequence_id
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://api.example/notifications?tenant=abc');
let last = 0;
ws.onmessage = (e) => {
  const m = JSON.parse(e.data);
  if (m.sequence_id !== last + 1) throw new Error('out of order');
  last = m.sequence_id;
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify deduplication if the same event arrives twice?
- What metrics would you collect to demonstrate backpressure handling and SLA adherence?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests must validate complex asynchronous flows, idempotency, webhook security, and failure resilience in a distributed system.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys across batch submissions
- Exactly-once processing guarantees with dedup stores
- Webhook signature validation and replay protection
- Retry/backoff strategies and dead-letter queues
- End-to-end latency and visibility across workers

## Code Example
```javascript
function verifySignature(payload, sig, secret) {
  const h = require('crypto').createHmac('sha256', secret);
  h.update(payload, 'utf8');
  const digest = h.digest('hex');
  return require('crypto').timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(digest), Buffer.from(sig));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you simulate DLQ behavior in CI/CD?
- What metrics and logs would you correlate to detect idempotency issues?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests production-like asynchronous data paths and cross-region consistency, with failure modes that surface race conditions and retry logic.

## Key Concepts
- Asynchronous batch processing
- Eventual consistency across regions
- Idempotency and replay safety
- Observability: traces, metrics, logs
- Failure modes: MQ, workers, storage

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: polling for report readiness
async function waitForReport(token){
  while(true){
    const r = await fetch(`/tenant/a/reports/${token}`)
    if(r.status===200 && r.json().state==='READY') return r.json()
    await sleep(1000)
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you isolate data per tenant in tests? How to simulate cross-region read replicas?
- How would you validate back-pressure handling under burst traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
This question targets practical API testing of asynchronous flows, idempotency, and webhook reliability—common in real systems.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys for POST safety
- Async polling and state progression
- Webhook delivery and retry/backoff
- Negative paths and concurrency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch for idempotency
async function testIdempotentPost() {
  const key = 'idemp-123';
  const a = await post('/orders', {items:[1,2]}, {headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  const b = await post('/orders', {items:[1,2]}, {headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  assert(a.status===202 && b.status===202);
  assert(a.body.order_id===b.body.order_id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate webhook retries and idempotent processing across failures?
- What tooling would you use to mock downstream services and observe end-to-end retry behavior?

## Why This Is Asked

Streaming APIs combined with REST surfaces are common in large-scale systems. This question probes testing of delivery guarantees, ordering, resilience under partial outages, and observability—areas critical for Snap, Microsoft, and Google-scale services.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing across REST and WebSocket channels
- Message ordering and sequencing guarantees
- Delivery semantics (at-least-once vs exactly-once) and replay safety
- Reconnection, backoff, and backfill strategies using durable offsets
- Handling partial upstream outages and dynamic schema changes
- Observability with tracing, logs, and metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: basic WebSocket stream ordering test (conceptual)
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://example.com/stream');
let lastSeq = -1;
let ok = true;
let count = 0;
ws.on('message', (data) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(data);
  if (typeof msg.seq !== 'number' || msg.seq <= lastSeq) ok = false;
  lastSeq = msg.seq;
  count++;
  if (count >= 100) ws.close();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate network partitions and validate reconnect/backoff behavior across regions?
- How would you validate exactly-once semantics when an event is replayed during backfill?

## Why This Is Asked

Examines ability to test dynamic, central policy-driven routing at scale and under failure modes.

## Key Concepts

- Central policy propagation across distributed nodes
- Race conditions during config reloads
- Per-tenant overrides and canary validation
- Observability, metrics, and alerting for policy health

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: verify policy reloads propagate within short window
async function testPolicyPropagation(nodePool, policyApi) {
  await policyApi.forceUpdate();
  const results = await Promise.all(nodePool.map(n => n.getCurrentPolicyVersion()));
  return results.every(v => v === results[0]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate policy service latency and measure impact on SLAs?
- What tooling would you use to replay policy updates in canary regions?

## Why This Is Asked

End-to-end tests for asynchronous batch APIs ensure reliability where results arrive via webhooks and not immediately. It tests idempotency, security of callbacks, partial successes, and retry/backfill strategies under realistic traffic.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent batch submission
- Webhook validation (signature)
- Eventual consistency and SLA
- Partial failures and per-record status transitions
- Replay/backfill of results

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: validate webhook signature during callback
const crypto = require('crypto');
function validWebhook(payload, sig, secret){
  const h = crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret).update(payload).digest('hex');
  return crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(sig), Buffer.from(h));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate bursty webhook delivery and ensure ordering guarantees?
- How would you test idempotency keys at scale with distributed queues?
